,"paper":"42","sha256":"ea108d62044d9875829c5d40370d45642d78f217f68b9f48a5b8a81d7787b05c","source_url":"https://pastpapers.papacambridge.com/directories/CAIE/CAIE-pastpapers/upload/9702_m24_ms_42.pdf","local_path":"papers/cambridge/9702/2024/9702_m24_ms_42--ea108d6204.pdf","level":"external","qualification":"9702","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025","paper_label":"42"}},"given_text":null}],"contexts":[]}],"guidance":{"entries":{"duplicate-b0b817bbad4e52f3":{"hint":"Write the required quantity in terms of the measurements, then track how each measurement’s uncertainty contributes.","notes":["For products and quotients, add fractional uncertainties; for powers, multiply fractional uncertainty by the magnitude of the power. For sums and differences, combine absolute uncertainties using the stated worst-case convention.","For a graph gradient, use the steepest and shallowest acceptable lines through the error bars. Report an uncertainty with matching precision to the quantity, not extra calculator digits.","Link the relevant physical cause to the observation asked about. Naming a law alone does not explain why this particular result follows."]},"duplicate-7a4b51176682d6e1":{"hint":"Write the required quantity in terms of the measurements, then track how each measurement’s uncertainty contributes.","notes":["For products and quotients, add fractional uncertainties; for powers, multiply fractional uncertainty by the magnitude of the power. For sums and differences, combine absolute uncertainties using the stated worst-case convention.","For a graph gradient, use the steepest and shallowest acceptable lines through the error bars. Report an uncertainty with matching precision to the quantity, not extra calculator digits.","Use any result supplied above directly where needed. You do not need to redo an earlier calculation from another topic."]},"duplicate-e5eeb1b2213df6bd":{"hint":"Distinguish a prediction for one nucleus from a statistical prediction for a large collection.","notes":["Radioactive decay is spontaneous and random: the decay time of one nucleus cannot be predicted, but a large sample follows a statistical decay law.","Alpha, beta and gamma radiation differ in charge, ionization and penetration. Relate the stated observation to the physical property being tested, rather than treating all radiation as identical.","Use any result supplied above directly where needed. You do not need to redo an earlier calculation from another topic."]},"duplicate-50da090ce2c80e83":{"hint":"Keep a separate account of nucleon number and electric charge on both sides of the decay.","notes":["In alpha decay A decreases by 4 and Z by 2. In beta-minus decay a neutron becomes a proton; in beta-plus decay a proton becomes a neutron, with the appropriate emitted leptons.","Gamma emission changes nuclear energy without changing A or Z. Beta decay shares energy with a neutrino or antineutrino, giving a continuous beta spectrum."]},"duplicate-ada7ce8b3280e0d5":{"hint":"Compare the total mass or binding energy of the initial particles with that of the final particles.","notes":["Energy released is (mass_initial − mass_final)c². Equivalently, it is the increase in total binding energy, not simply the difference in binding energy per nucleon.","When given binding energy per nucleon, multiply by the nucleon number first. Keep atomic and nuclear masses consistent so electron masses cancel correctly.","For “show that”, start from the supplied data and display the intermediate steps. Do not substitute the result you are asked to show as an assumption."]},"duplicate-84796d4356def6a4":{"hint":"Compare the total mass or binding energy of the initial particles with that of the final particles.","notes":["Energy released is (mass_initial − mass_final)c². Equivalently, it is the increase in total binding energy, not simply the difference in binding energy per nucleon.","When given binding energy per nucleon, multiply by the nucleon number first. Keep atomic and nuclear masses consistent so electron masses cancel correctly.","For “show that”, start from the supplied data and display the intermediate steps. Do not substitute the result you are asked to show as an assumption."]},"duplicate-7c0a11b0c1faed92":{"hint":"Separate what the common small deflections tell you from what the rare large deflections tell you.","notes":["Alpha scattering supports a small, dense, positively charged nucleus containing most of the atomic mass. Most particles pass through the largely empty atomic volume.","For a head-on closest-approach calculation, equate the initial kinetic energy to the increase in electric potential energy, using the alpha particle’s charge +2e."]},"duplicate-3be839a32b3434ad":{"hint":"Separate what the common small deflections tell you from what the rare large deflections tell you.","notes":["Alpha scattering supports a small, dense, positively charged nucleus containing most of the atomic mass. Most particles pass through the largely empty atomic volume.","For a head-on closest-approach calculation, equate the initial kinetic energy to the increase in electric potential energy, using the alpha particle’s charge +2e."]},"ibd-ca2198e848a9d5c8-q5:b.iii":{"hint":"Separate what the common small deflections tell you from what the rare large deflections tell you.","notes":["Alpha scattering supports a small, dense, positively charged nucleus containing most of the atomic mass. Most particles pass through the largely empty atomic volume.","For a head-on closest-approach calculation, equate the initial kinetic energy to the increase in electric potential energy, using the alpha particle’s charge +2e."]},"ibd-ca2198e848a9d5c8-q5:b.iv":{"hint":"Keep the proton count, neutron count and total nucleon count separate.","notes":["Isotopes share proton number Z but differ in neutron number. Stability depends on the neutron-to-proton balance and competition between nuclear attraction and proton repulsion.","Nuclear radius scales approximately as A^(1/3), giving roughly constant nuclear density. Outer electrons control chemical behaviour; the nucleus controls radioactive properties."]},"ibd-f3a8fcdae81984dc-q26:whole":{"hint":"Compare how nuclear mass and nuclear volume depend on nucleon number.","notes":["Nuclear radius is approximately R = R₀A^(1/3), while nuclear mass is approximately proportional to A. Volume therefore scales with A.","A is nucleon number and Z is proton number; neutron number is A − Z. Do not confuse the radius of the nucleus with the much larger atomic radius."]},"duplicate-240574048c61794f":{"hint":"Keep a separate account of nucleon number and electric charge on both sides of the decay.","notes":["In alpha decay A decreases by 4 and Z by 2. In beta-minus decay a neutron becomes a proton; in beta-plus decay a proton becomes a neutron, with the appropriate emitted leptons.","Gamma emission changes nuclear energy without changing A or Z. Beta decay shares energy with a neutrino or antineutrino, giving a continuous beta spectrum."]},"cross-aq24p2-32:whole":{"hint":"Compare the elapsed time with the half-life, or express the remaining amount as a fraction of the initial amount.","notes":["N = N₀e^(−λt), A = λN, and T_half = ln 2/λ. After n half-lives, the remaining fraction is (½)^n.","Subtract background from measured count rates before finding a decay ratio. Activity counts decays per second; it is not the total number of undecayed nuclei."],"method":"radioactive-law","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-oc24p2-7:whole":{"hint":"Compare the elapsed time with the half-life, or express the remaining amount as a fraction of the initial amount.","notes":["N = N₀e^(−λt), A = λN, and T_half = ln 2/λ. After n half-lives, the remaining fraction is (½)^n.","Subtract background from measured count rates before finding a decay ratio. Activity counts decays per second; it is not the total number of undecayed nuclei."],"method":"radioactive-law","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ca24m4-written-8biii:whole":{"hint":"What changes when the mass number increases but the proton number stays fixed?","notes":["The neutron number is A − Z. Stability depends on the neutron–proton balance; a larger nucleus is not automatically more stable."],"method":"nuclear-stability","review_status":"reviewed"}}},"answers":{"entries":{}},"presentation":{"practice/parts/ibd-6c4f514567670756-q3-b.i-875de48832924641.webp":{"crop":[0,5,1051,208],"size":[1051,230]},"practice/parts/ibd-6c4f514567670756-q3-b.ii-46459c1572032ba0.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1051,290],"size":[1051,311]},"practice/parts/ibd-5b2623ffb5182b99-q3-b.ii-1fbf2d66dfa5c203.webp":{"crop":[0,0,1545,441],"size":[1545,622]},"practice/parts/ibd-6c4f514567670756-q3-c-db385eb38395bc37.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1051,463],"size":[1051,698]},"practice/parts/ibd-5b2623ffb5182b99-q3-c-700e3d7d02306cd2.webp":{"crop":[0,0,1545,865],"size":[1545,876]},"practice/parts/ibd-6c4f514567670756-q3-stem-e895308cb5d0fe5c.webp":{"crop":[0,5,1051,649],"size":[1051,672],"masks":[[14,5,21,33]]},"practice/parts/ibd-6c4f514567670756-q3-stem-32a38a68962ddc57.webp":{"crop":[0,20,1051,775],"size":[1051,812],"masks":[[13,0,244,3]]},"practice/parts/ibd-6c4f514567670756-q3-context-b-d9d5e14092d5104f.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1051,116],"size":[1051,132]},"practice/parts/ibd-ca2198e848a9d5c8-q5-a.i-9568da98ca6725c6.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1051,183],"size":[1051,203]},"practice/parts/ibd-ca2198e848a9d5c8-q5-a.ii-3cfd2d281bb5d7c8.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1051,264],"size":[1051,284]},"practice/parts/ibd-ca2198e848a9d5c8-q5-a.iii-f3abc03d7271f01a.webp":{"crop":[0,5,1051,263],"size":[1051,279]},"practice/parts/ibd-ca2198e848a9d5c8-q5-b.i-00604427b462b307.webp":{"crop":[0,5,1051,264],"size":[1051,285]},"practice/parts/ibd-ca2198e848a9d5c8-q5-b.ii-cbc2d0687cea7642.webp":{"crop":[0,5,1051,263],"size":[1051,298]},"practice/parts/ibd-ca2198e848a9d5c8-q5-b.iv-3dfc1db626787025.webp":{"crop":[0,1,1051,309],"size":[10